test/check/error-guards.sumspec:15:27:
   |
15 | wider :: Page.Home // not Link.Off -> Page.Plan
   |                           ^
a guard describes the same part of the state as the source, so it starts with Page, not Link; to look at a wider part, start the transition with a shared path that holds both, as P => ...

test/check/error-guards.sumspec:16:38:
   |
16 | element :: App => Page.Home // Files[i](Kind.Text) -> Page.Plan
   |                                      ^
unknown name i; name a value with #i in the input or on the source side

test/check/error-guards.sumspec:17:23:
   |
17 | list :: Page.Home // [File] -> Page.Plan
   |                       ^
a guard describes a state, not a list type; write the name of the list's type

test/check/error-guards.sumspec:18:32:
   |
18 | unknown :: App => Page.Home // Missing -> Page.Plan
   |                                ^
Missing is not a factor of App (Page * Link * Files * A)

test/check/error-guards.sumspec:19:39:
   |
19 | arguments :: App => Page.Home // Page.Home(A) -> Page.Devices
   |                                       ^
Page.Home takes no arguments

test/check/error-guards.sumspec:20:49:
   |
20 | named Id#i :: App => Page.Home // Files[i](Kind#k) -> Page.Plan
   |                                                 ^
a guard binds no value, so #k names nothing; name the value in the input or on the source side

test/check/error-guards.sumspec:21:49:
   |
21 | primed Id#i :: App => Page.Home // Files[i](Kind') -> Page.Plan
   |                                                 ^
a prime (') marks a new value, so it can only be on the target side

